    },
    {
      "category": "open-redirect",
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "files": ["src/redirect.js"],
      "evidence": "`continueTo` calls `res.redirect(req.query.next)` with no validation or allow-listing of the `next` parameter. An attacker can construct a phishing URL such as `https://trusted-host/continue?next=https://evil.com`; the trusted host issues a 302 to the attacker-controlled destination, lending credibility to credential theft or malware delivery. Because the redirect target is taken verbatim from user input, relative-path and absolute-URL redirection are both possible.",
      "remediation": "Validate `req.query.next` against an allow-list of trusted internal paths/origins, require relative paths only (reject values starting with `//` or an `http(s)://` scheme), and return a 400 for disallowed targets. Alternatively, map an opaque token to a known safe destination rather than accepting arbitrary URLs."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": ["src/config.js", "src/health.js", "src/redirect.js"],
  "source_modified": false
}
"}

